{"name":"Learning Green","hex":"#abc123","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"learning","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act in which something is learned.","Accumulated knowledge."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of learn"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/learning"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Learning","blocks":[{"text":"Learning is the process of acquiring new understanding, knowledge, behavior, skills, values, attitudes, and preferences. The ability to learn is possessed by humans, other animals, and some machines. There is also evidence for some kind of learning in certain plants. Some learning is immediate, induced by a single event, but much skill and knowledge accumulate from repeated experiences. The changes induced by learning often last a lifetime, and it is hard to distinguish learned material that seems to be \"lost\" from that which cannot be retrieved."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Cooking_contest_140418-N-OX321-101.jpg/330px-Cooking_contest_140418-N-OX321-101.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning","wikidata":"Q133500"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Children_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg/500px-Children_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Children_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg/1280px-Children_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Children_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg/500px-Children_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Children learn to bike in the eighties in Czechoslovakia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChildren_competition_on_side_wheels_in_the_eighties_in_Czechoslovakia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9